  "slug": "galaxy-clustering-cc66",
  "summary": "Preprints use TNG, HR4, HESTIA and baryonified N-body runs to quantify biases in H0, w0wa, fNL and assembly-bias inference for Euclid- and DESI-era analyses.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of simulations to calibrate systematics and secondary dependencies between observables and cosmological parameters. Among nuisance effects, on-the-fly Gaussian Process reconstruction of n(z) in Euclid-like 3×2pt analyses shows that redshift-distribution errors bias w0wa. IllustrisTNG ellipticals indicate that multipole mass terms bias time-delay H0 by 1–6%. Light-cone and relativistic effects bias local fNL by about 2σ in a Euclid-like survey and by more than 10σ in a MegaMapper-like survey. Baryonification V generates tSZ Compton-y maps from dark-matter-only light cones. For secondary bias, TNG300 attributes halo secondary bias largely to the unbound matter fraction in the circum-halo medium. Luminosity-weighted marked correlations are proposed as an assembly-bias null test on DESI-BGS mocks. In observation–simulation comparisons, SDSS genus topology agrees with modern HR4 galaxy models, and Local Group satellite-pair statistics are compared with HESTIA. More peripheral work includes a GR test using DESI Legacy cluster clustering and a Gini-index analysis of BCG dominance that finds no evolution.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
